<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  NO encoding declaration, deliberately, and it is the one line here worth reading twice. This
  template is rendered TWICE into two different encodings: CMake writes UTF-8 (for the build-tree
  copy the servicecheck arm greps and validates), and the installer's PowerShell writes UTF-16LE
  with a BOM (which is what schtasks reliably accepts). Any encoding= this file could name would
  be a lie about one of them - and a declaration that disagrees with the bytes is refused by
  schtasks as "The task XML contains a value which is incorrectly formatted or out of range",
  which names neither the encoding nor a line. With no declaration, XML's own rules make both
  renderings correct: a BOM selects UTF-16, and its absence defaults to UTF-8.
-->
<!--
  naudio - Task Scheduler LOGON TASK for na_audio_daemon (issue #96 item 2, Windows half).
  SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-or-later

  Generated by CMake from this template, then RE-GENERATED by the NSIS installer against the
  directory the operator actually chose. Two renderings, deliberately: CMake's names the path
  this build installs to and is what the servicecheck arm asserts against, while the installer's
  names $INSTDIR, which the operator can change on the installer's directory page. macOS and
  Linux need only one because their package prefixes are fixed; Windows' is not.

  WHY A LOGON TASK AND NOT A SERVICE. A real SCM service is the wrong shape here, for the same
  reason a LaunchDaemon is on macOS and a systemd system unit is on Linux, one layer down:
  Windows services run in session 0, which has no audio endpoints. One would install cleanly,
  start cleanly, and then capture nothing, with nothing an operator could fix. A logon task runs
  in the interactive session, where the audio devices are.

  THREE DEFAULTS THAT WOULD BREAK THIS SILENTLY, all set explicitly below:
    - ExecutionTimeLimit defaults to three days (PT72H). A service runs until it is stopped, so
      it must be PT0S - the spelling for "no limit".
    - DisallowStartIfOnBatteries defaults TRUE, and StopIfGoingOnBatteries with it. On a laptop
      the task would refuse to start, or stop mid-stream when the charger came out.
    - MultipleInstancesPolicy defaults to IgnoreNew, which is what we want: logging in twice
      must not start a second daemon fighting for the same control port.

  Task Scheduler does NOT redirect stdout/stderr, and the command below is the WINDOWED build,
  na_audio_daemonw.exe, which has no console at all: it writes its own log to
  %LOCALAPPDATA%\naudio\daemon.log (the launchd agent's log file, one platform over) and shows
  a tray icon. A config-file error is there, on the control page, and in the task's
  LastTaskResult (exit 2). The man page says so.

  Enabled is FALSE: installing a package must not start capturing at the next logon. The NSIS
  section registers the task disabled and the operator enables it, which is the same property
  systemd's not-enabled state gives Linux. macOS is the odd one out (issue #102): it has a system
  switch for background items, Login Items, which disagrees with a Disabled key, so the launchd
  agent ships without one and is inert by its arguments instead. Windows has no such switch.

  The Description names the service as every platform names it - "Network Audio Service", the
  string tools/CMakeLists.txt owns as NAUDIO_APP_NAME and the Start-menu shortcut, the Linux
  desktop entry and the macOS app all carry. It is a LITERAL here, not an @-token, because of the
  second rendering above: the installer's PowerShell substitutes the program path and nothing
  else, so a token would reach the registered task unreplaced. The servicecheck arm asserts the
  CMake rendering's description contains NAUDIO_APP_NAME, which is what keeps this literal and
  that variable one string. The task's own path stays \naudio\naudio-daemon: it is what the
  documented schtasks commands, the daemon's own query and the uninstaller name.
-->
<Task version="1.2" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/windows/2004/02/mit/task">
  <RegistrationInfo>
    <URI>\naudio\naudio-daemon</URI>
    <Description>Network Audio Service: runs the naudio audio streaming daemon and its local control page at logon.</Description>
  </RegistrationInfo>
  <Triggers>
    <LogonTrigger>
      <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    </LogonTrigger>
  </Triggers>
  <Principals>
    <Principal id="Author">
      <GroupId>S-1-5-32-545</GroupId>
      <RunLevel>LeastPrivilege</RunLevel>
    </Principal>
  </Principals>
  <Settings>
    <Enabled>false</Enabled>
    <MultipleInstancesPolicy>IgnoreNew</MultipleInstancesPolicy>
    <DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>false</DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>
    <StopIfGoingOnBatteries>false</StopIfGoingOnBatteries>
    <AllowHardTerminate>true</AllowHardTerminate>
    <StartWhenAvailable>false</StartWhenAvailable>
    <RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable>false</RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable>
    <IdleSettings>
      <StopOnIdleEnd>false</StopOnIdleEnd>
      <RestartOnIdle>false</RestartOnIdle>
    </IdleSettings>
    <AllowStartOnDemand>true</AllowStartOnDemand>
    <Hidden>false</Hidden>
    <RunOnlyIfIdle>false</RunOnlyIfIdle>
    <WakeToRun>false</WakeToRun>
    <ExecutionTimeLimit>PT0S</ExecutionTimeLimit>
    <Priority>7</Priority>
  </Settings>
  <Actions Context="Author">
    <Exec>
      <Command>@NAUDIO_SERVICE_EXEC@</Command>
      <Arguments>--mode control --duration-ms 0</Arguments>
    </Exec>
  </Actions>
</Task>
